{"records":[{"id":"kdfvg2rvjn","title":"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex","description":"the raw data of all replicates conducted for the project \"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T14:13:31.651Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kdfvg2rvjn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kdfvg2rvjn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yilun","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Topoisomerase","Colorectal Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c52ypw995y","title":"A Ketogenic Diet Sensitizes Pancreatic Cancer to Glutamine Metabolism Inhibitors","description":"Attached below are the metabolomics and RNA-sequencing datasets associated with the manuscript titled ‘A Ketogenic Diet Sensitizes Pancreatic Cancer to Glutamine Metabolism Inhibitors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-23T19:50:14.697Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c52ypw995y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c52ypw995y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Omid","last_name":"Hajihassani","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v6cszczy48","title":"Kinase Inhibitors Screen Tamoxifen Resistant Breast Cancer","description":"Antiestrogen resistance of breast cancer has been related to enhanced growth factor receptor expression and activation. We have previously shown that ectopic expression and subsequent activation of the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R) or the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) in MCF7 or T47D breast cancer cells results in antiestrogen resistance. In order to identify critical therapeutic targets that mediate this antiestrogen resistance, we performed a kinase inhibitor screen with 273 different inhibitors. Various ALK inhibitors, including TEA684, AZD3463 and LDK378, inhibited cell proliferation in IGF1R expressing cells under both normal and antiestrogen resistance conditions by preventing IGF1R activation and subsequent downstream signaling; ALK inhibitors did not affect EGFR signaling. On the other hand, MEK1/2 inhibitors, including PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733, selectively antagonized IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance but did not affect cell proliferation under normal growth conditions. The MEK inhibitors PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733 caused an arrest in the G0/G1 cell cycle phase only under antiestrogen resistance conditions in IGF1R expressing cells, but not under normal growth conditions. RNAseq analysis revealed that MEK inhibitors PD0325901 and selumetinib drastically altered cell cycle progression and cell migration networks under IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance. In a group of 291 patients with primary operable ER+ breast cancer, strong pMEK staining in metastatic breast cancer after first-line tamoxifen treatment was significantly related to no clinical benefit. We propose a critical role for MEK activation in IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance and anticipate that dual-targeted therapy with MEK inhibitor and antiestrogen could improve treatment outcome.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-06T16:25:15.469Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v6cszczy48","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v6cszczy48"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Meerman","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Estrogen","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Tamoxifen"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rjxfxzsh5m","title":"DATA for C67 represses DNA repair","description":"The raw data for the paper entitled \"Elevated CXorf67 Expression in PFA Ependymomas Represses DNA Repair and Sensitizes the Tumors to PARP inhibitors\" in Cancer Cell.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-20T00:46:01.34Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rjxfxzsh5m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rjxfxzsh5m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jichang","last_name":"han","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cdrj2vrv8f","title":"SUPERTAM_HGU133A Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) in term of lymph node and distant metastasis","description":"Background\nLymph node status is generally considered as one of the best prognostic factors in breast cancer. However, association between nodal and distant metastasis is not straightforward. Here we analyze differences between molecular mechanisms responsible for nodal and distant metastasis, and the impact of molecular subtype on this association. \nMethods\nWe use SUPERTAM_HGU133A cohort of 836 patients with full microarray data available. Logistic regression evaluates distant metastasis risk, and Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) is used to identify molecular mechanisms targetable in the key clinical scenarios.\nResults\nGSEA shows different molecular background for lymph node and distant metastasis and unique patterns of deregulated molecular processes in tumors of four breast cancer subtypes. Risk of distant metastasis in node positive luminal A patients is strong in SUPERTAM_HGU133A (OR: 2.401, CI: 1.316-4.380) dataset. For luminal A tumors, nodal positivity is associated with enrichment of NF-κB and Src, while distant metastasis is associated with strong mechanisms of cell cycle regulation, thrombolysis, DNA-repair, and immune response. Based on GSEA results, we select panels of promising inhibitors applicable for the key clinical scenarios depending on lymph node status that are currently being tested in vitro, in vivo, or in clinical trials.\nConclusions\nPotential molecular targets are different for nodal and distant metastasis in breast cancer and are highly variable among different molecular subtypes. Panels of inhibitors have potential to improve the outcome of luminal A breast cancer patients based on lymph node status. We hope that further clinical trials have potential to translate the current knowledge from the laboratory to an improved treatment of breast cancer patients.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-25T13:26:58.186Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cdrj2vrv8f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cdrj2vrv8f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Petr","last_name":"Lapcik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Pospisilova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pavel","last_name":"Bouchal","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Gene Expression","Metastasis","Breast Cancer Subtyping","Lymph Node"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m4s288hg57","title":"Association Between Cyclin-Dependent Kinase 4/6 Inhibitors and Nephrotoxicity in Patients with Breast Cancer: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis","description":"This study conducted a meta-analysis of 17 randomized controlled trials involving 19,638 patients to assess the risk of nephrotoxicity associated with cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitors in breast cancer treatment. The analysis found a significant increase in all-grade nephrotoxic adverse events (AEs), including elevated blood creatinine, acute kidney injury, and renal impairment (RR = 3.12, 95% CI [2.11, 4.63]). The incidence of grade 3 or higher nephrotoxicity was also notably higher in treated patients (RR = 3.12, 95% CI [1.74, 5.58]). Subgroup analyses indicated differential risks across the four CDK4/6 inhibitors. These results underscore the importance of clinicians closely monitoring renal function in patients receiving CDK4/6 inhibitors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-07T14:03:56.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m4s288hg57","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m4s288hg57"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Guoshuang","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiayong","last_name":"Cui","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Cyclin-Dependent Kinase","Meta-Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nzsk26yf5x","title":"ABL Kinases Regulate Translation in HER2+ Cells through Y-Box-Binding Protein 1 to Facilitate Colonization of the Brain McKernan et al","description":"Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2-positive (HER2/ERBB2) breast cancer patients often present with brain metastasis. HER2-targeted therapies have not been successful to treat brain metastases in part due to poor blood-brain barrier (BBB) penetrance and emergence of resistance. Here we report that ABL kinase allosteric inhibitors improve overall survival and impair HER2+ brain metastatic outgrowth in vivo. Mechanistically, ABL kinases phosphorylate the RNA binding protein Y-box-binding protein 1 (YB-1). ABL kinase inhibition disrupts binding of YB-1 to the ERBB2 mRNA and impairs translation, leading to a profound decrease in HER2 protein levels. ABL-dependent tyrosine phosphorylation of YB-1 promotes HER2 translation. Notably, loss of YB-1 inhibits brain metastatic outgrowth and impairs expression of a subset of ABL-dependent brain metastatic targets. These data support a role for ABL kinases in the translational regulation of brain metastatic targets through YB-1 and offer a therapeutic target for HER2+ brain metastasis patients. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-30T18:28:16.164Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nzsk26yf5x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nzsk26yf5x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Courtney","last_name":"McKernan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Brain Metastasis","RNA-Binding Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"48pfmwbcb5","title":"Synergistic effect of capsaicin and docetaxel in prostate cancer cells through AMP-activated kinase","description":"Data used to generate figures of the paper “Combination of the natural product capsaicin and docetaxel synergistically kills human prostate cancer cells through the metabolic regulator AMP-activated kinase” Sánchez BG, Bort A, Mateos-Gómez PA, Rodríguez-Henche N, Díaz-Laviada I. Cancer Cell Int. 2019 Mar 8;19:54. doi: 10.1186/s12935-019-0769-2. eCollection 2019.\nIn this study we demonstrate that the spicy ingredient of peepers, capsaicin, sensitizes prostate cancer cell to docetaxel. The metabolic regulator AMPK plays a pivotal role.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-05-29T15:24:03.174Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/48pfmwbcb5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/48pfmwbcb5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ines","last_name":"Diaz-Laviada","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Belen","last_name":"Sanchez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Bort","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pedro","last_name":"Mateos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nieves","last_name":"Rodríguez-Henche","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Prostate Cancer","Docetaxel","Capsaicin","Synergism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xjwbwrb35k","title":"The Receptor Tyrosine Kinase AXL is Required at Multiple Steps of the Metastatic Cascade during HER2-positive Breast Cancer Progression","description":"Western blots from The Receptor Tyrosine Kinase AXL is Required at Multiple Steps of the Metastatic Cascade during HER2-positive Breast Cancer Progression","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-19T15:51:16.129Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xjwbwrb35k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xjwbwrb35k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jean-Francois","last_name":"Côté","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zr5dwm5y3p","title":"BCSD-2024V2","description":"Among women, breast cancer is a prominent cancer type. Like any other type of cancer, the most crucial aspects influencing the prognosis of breast cancer are early identification and detection. With the use of computers, screening based on mammography provides help in the detection process of breast cancer at an initial stage. Many researchers have examined and put forth techniques for computer-aided diagnosis (CADx) of anomalies associated with breast cancer in mammography throughout the previous fifteen years. CADx frameworks are designed to assist radiologists in providing second opinions regarding breast lesion detection. Big and varied datasets are necessary for training the model of machine learning. They increase the accuracy and resilience of the CADx system by teaching it to recognize patterns linked to breast cancer. Researchers may create and test new image processing and machine learning approaches using comprehensive and well-annotated mammography datasets. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-08T06:27:21.757Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zr5dwm5y3p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zr5dwm5y3p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JAVARIA","last_name":"Amin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shagufta","last_name":"Manzoor","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Image Segmentation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129166,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":116,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129166,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128664,"restricted":63,"embargoed":439},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2159},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1502},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":590},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":457},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":429},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":408},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":297},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":256}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":126564},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1897},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":183},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","nam